## Common Request Handling

- "Criar storyboard desse projeto": confirm/infer `9:16` or `16:9`, map assets, create director brief, per-frame prompts, and GPT Image storyboard board prompt.
- "Fazer pacote completo": create index, scene boards, detail boards, film-sequence board, keyframe plan, prompts, and revision notes.
- "Transformar em video": evaluate approved storyboard/keyframes first, then write Seedance prompt.
- "Usar este mundo": treat the world handoff as creative direction and real assets as geometry/material truth.
- "Adicionar pessoas": load character rules; keep people secondary to architecture unless the user wants presenter/lifestyle-led content.
- "Detalhar arquitetura": use technical callouts as presentation annotations unless the user explicitly asks for construction documentation.

## Fast Start Behavior

When the user provides assets or a world handoff and asks for a storyboard, film, video prompt, Seedance prompt, launch reel, sales presentation, or says to start, proceed directly into the staged workflow.

If no assets are attached, decide whether the user wants a conceptual storyboard from scratch or strict real-project fidelity:

- For conceptual work, proceed with clear assumptions and label it conceptual.
- For real-project fidelity with no assets, ask for renders/photos/plans plus aspect ratio, platform, and duration.
- If only one non-essential detail is missing, use a sensible default and state it briefly.

Default assumptions when not specified:

- Aspect ratio: `9:16` for social/mobile; `16:9` for presentation/horizontal. Ask or state the inferred choice.
- Duration: 30 seconds for social videos, with 8-14 visually different shots.
- Output phase: GPT Image storyboard prompt first; final Seedance prompt only after the generated board/keyframes are shared, unless the user asks for provisional video prompt.
- People: none unless requested.
- Board text: short labels and timecodes only.

## Workflow

1. Confirm or infer aspect ratio: `9:16` or `16:9`.
2. Identify project type, platform, duration, audience, and whether the output is a storyboard plan, complete project package, or video prompt.
3. Identify every uploaded asset by exact file name and classify it by space/use.
4. Select usage mode: finished project images, conceptual storyboard from scratch, or `create-real-estate-worlds` handoff.
5. Load only the references required by the request.
6. Build an environment continuity map: canonical asset/concept for each space, fixed material/furniture anchors, light logic, adjacent-space logic, and what must not change.
7. Build a short director brief: objective, audience, storytelling promise, hook, narrative arc, photorealism target, aspect ratio, duration, pacing, camera style, transition style, sound cues, and continuity anchors.
8. Choose a narrative pattern and campaign structure. For 30-second social videos, prefer 8-14 shots split across multiple boards.
9. Create a shot-to-file or shot-to-concept map. Every shot must come from an uploaded file, safe crop, safe close-up, clean keyframe, or clearly defined concept prompt.
10. Write per-frame GPT Image prompts before the storyboard board prompt.
11. Write GPT Image storyboard board prompts with reference map, shot descriptions, local timecodes, movement notes, selected aspect ratio, and negative constraints.
12. For project packages, create individual scene boards, architectural detail boards, mood/visual DNA sections, and a 4 to 6 frame film-sequence board as needed.
13. Stop after storyboard/keyframe prompts unless the user already shared generated boards/keyframes or asks for a provisional Seedance prompt.
14. After seeing generated boards/keyframes, evaluate fidelity, realism, continuity, repeated-frame risk, text clutter, and Seedance failure points before writing final video prompts.

## Project Package Workflow

Use this when the user wants a broader professional package rather than only one video storyboard prompt.

1. Read `references/project-storyboard-package.md`.
2. Read `references/architectural-detailing-menu.md` when detail boards or technical callouts are needed.
3. Build a project storyboard index by facade, room, amenity, aerial, detail, lifestyle, and film sequence.
4. Create individual scene boards for approved images or planned scenes.
5. Add moodboard/visual DNA sections only when they clarify materials, light, camera language, and continuity.
6. Choose 3 to 7 relevant architectural detail topics per major approved image.
7. Create at least one 4 to 6 frame film-sequence board when video storytelling is part of the request.
8. End with revision notes: what is approved, what must be regenerated, what needs extra references, and what should become permanent project rules.

