## Validation

Before finalizing:

1. Confirm the project uses `9:16` or `16:9`, and that all images, panels, keyframes, and video prompts match the chosen ratio.
2. Check that all references are either strict source assets or clearly labeled concept inspiration.
3. Check that architecture, materials, layout, furniture, landscape, and views are preserved where real assets exist.
4. Check that no unsupported reverse angles, invented spaces, repeated frames, random people, or changed materials appear.
5. Check that every shot has a purpose and that the sequence tells a real estate story.
6. Check that final video prompts animate the architectural scene, not the storyboard board page.

## Success Criteria

The storyboard is successful when it can guide image generation, project presentation, and later Seedance video generation while preserving the exact architectural identity, selected aspect ratio, visual continuity, and professional real estate tone.

## Current Client-Value Overrides

These rules supersede any older generic package behavior in this skill.

- Separate client-facing deliverables from production-facing deliverables. Client-facing boards must sell, explain, or document design value; production storyboards must guide video generation.
- Do not create decorative editorial pages just because they look good. Every board must have a practical job for the client, portfolio, social content, or video pipeline.
- For client and portfolio value, prioritize material moodboards, room finish boards, architectural detail boards, and selected editorial project pages.
- For video generation value, prioritize shot plans, production storyboards, clean keyframes, motion notes, and Seedance prompts.
- Material moodboards and finish boards are primary client-facing deliverables. They should use extracted materials, objects, furniture fragments, texture samples, swatches, vegetation, lighting pieces, ceramics, hardware, and finish labels.
- Generic Visual DNA pages and editorial storyboards are not default deliverables. Create them only when they clarify material logic, spatial continuity, brand/portfolio presentation, or the user explicitly asks for them.
- If an editorial page is not planning a video, call it an editorial project page, not a storyboard.
- Approved project images are source evidence, not mandatory repeated panels. Use them selectively as one hero crop, safe detail crops, extracted material/object fragments, or selected panels for spatial continuity/video sequence.
- Do not repeat all approved images as equal-weight grids unless the explicit goal is a production video storyboard.
- For Reels/social, the final page must be a true standard 9:16 vertical composition. Internal video panels must also remain true 9:16 rectangles, never thin strips, squeezed frames, squares, or mixed aspect ratios.
- Remove or revise any board that only rearranges approved images without adding client, portfolio, social, or production value.

Updated routing:

- Moodboard, material board, finish board, or prancha de materiais: create client-facing flat-lay or editorial material boards.
- Detalhamento, detail board, or technical board: create architectural detail boards with professional callouts.
- Storyboard para Seedance, video, Reels, or shot plan: create production video storyboards and clean keyframe plans.
- Editorial, revista, portfolio, or apresentacao: create selected editorial project pages with a clear role, not generic storyboard grids.
- Pacote completo: create a curated package with client-facing boards first and production boards only when video is part of the goal.

## Current Manual-Derived Image Sequence Overrides

Use `references/manual-derived-image-sequence-patterns.md` when the user sends training manuals, teacher PDFs, reference workflows, or asks for creative image/keyframe ideas for future Seedance architecture videos.

These rules are about still image generation and sequence logic only. Ignore any video-generation prompt text from the manuals. Do not copy instructor prompts, course wording, logos, or manual layouts.

- Treat the manuals as a pattern library for image types: facade-to-technical drawing, exploded architecture, mood/material overlay, close-up inserts, location/map/lot highlights, loteamento progressions, construction timelapse stages, lifestyle proof, and start/end frame pairs.
- When these creative video types are requested, create the image/keyframe plan first. Generate clean stills or GPT Image prompts before writing Seedance prompts.
- A single approved project image may produce 2-4 derivative images when useful: a close-up, a technical/explanatory frame, a mood/material overlay, or a transition keyframe. Preserve project consistency.
- Multi-reference composites are allowed only when the roles are clear: one source controls geometry, one controls material, one controls site/context, one controls technical information.
- For loteamento and timelapse work, prioritize realism, site-boundary consistency, and staged progression over flashy transitions.
- If the user says these creative formats are for later, add the logic to the skill/package but do not generate images until requested.

## Current Real Client Asset And Visual Hook Overrides

These rules coordinate with `create-real-estate-worlds`.

- Accept both core project images and strategic supplemental images from the world-building stage.
- Use core images to preserve project identity.
- Use supplemental images for moodboards, details, location proof, technical explanations, start/end frames, transitions, process/timelapse, loteamento progression, and strong video hooks.
- Do not assume every project needs start/end frames. Use them only when requested or when they clearly solve continuity, transformation, timelapse, loteamento, or transition needs.
- For real client projects, treat uploaded renders, SketchUp screenshots, photos, plans, drone images, and construction images as architectural truth. Preserve geometry, materials, floor count, fixed furniture, site boundaries, views, and scale.
- When creating new images from client assets, label whether each image is a strict derivative, safe crop/detail, technical overlay, mood/material board, start/end frame, or conceptual proposal.
- If a needed storyboard shot requires unsupported geometry or an unseen angle, request another reference or mark it as conceptual risk instead of inventing it silently.
- Every video storyboard must start with a strong visual hook, not merely a beautiful image. The hook may come from a core image or a supplemental image.

## Current Strategic Image Gap Library Override

Use `references/strategic-image-type-library.md` when reviewing approved images and deciding what extra images/keyframes may be missing for a stronger storyboard, client board, portfolio page, or Seedance pipeline.

Rules:

- Do not turn the library into a requirement to generate every image type.
- Use it to identify useful gaps: hook image, location proof, material/detail proof, technical explanation, spatial clarity, comfort diagram, lifestyle proof, match cut pair, or start/end pair.
- For real client projects, never invent unsupported geometry or hidden spaces. Mark missing references or ask for more assets.
- Start/end frame pairs are optional and should be used only when they solve a real transition or continuity need.
- The opening storyboard beat must be a strong visual hook, not merely a pleasant render.

